From abe444e73a12d118b6ae7937eb1e51ca2aeccf77 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: plotarmordev <299844489+plotarmordev@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 01:40:43 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 52/53] drama: voice encode checks a WAV's whole container and
 its resampling cost before reading samples

The clip's length at 44.1 kHz comes from the header, so an empty or over-60 s
clip is refused before any sample is decoded or resampled. A rate whose
resampling kernel would pass 2048 taps or 2^20 coefficients (44056 Hz needs
121M) is refused; every common rate up to 384 kHz fits. The RIFF size is
honoured: a file or chunk cut short of its declared size, and a data chunk
ending mid frame, are refused instead of read as a shorter clip; bytes after
the RIFF chunk stay unread. WAVE_FORMAT_EXTENSIBLE needs its 22-byte
extension, valid bits within the container (equal for float), a channel mask
without reserved bits or more speakers than channels, and the full PCM or
IEEE float subformat GUID. Accepted clips decode to the same samples as
before; a test pins their digest.
---
 docs/drama-voices.md            |   4 +-
 zig/src/families/drama/clip.zig | 437 +++++++++++++++++++++++++-------
 2 files changed, 354 insertions(+), 87 deletions(-)

diff --git a/docs/drama-voices.md b/docs/drama-voices.md
index a46cdca1..a704b2e5 100644
--- a/docs/drama-voices.md
+++ b/docs/drama-voices.md
@@ -53,7 +53,7 @@ python3 tools/zig/drama_voice.py --checkpoint DRAMA3_DIR --audio clips/NAME.wav
 - `--reference` runs the reference server's own encode for a `references[].audio_path`: `load_audio` at the codec's 44.1 kHz in mono, then `FishReferenceEncodeHook` under bfloat16 autocast on CUDA (the reference's default `reference_encode_dtype`). With `--device cpu` it encodes in float32. A 15.7 s clip takes about 2.6 GB of device memory and 10 to 20 s, most of it loading `codec.pth`.
 - Clips longer than 60 s are refused, as the reference refuses them. Leading and trailing white space in the transcript is dropped.
 
-Without Python, the native binary encodes a WAV clip (PCM or IEEE float, any rate, channels averaged) on CUDA:
+Without Python, the native binary encodes a WAV clip (PCM or IEEE float, channels averaged) on CUDA:
 
 ```sh
 tensorfold-native voice encode clip.wav --model DRAMA3_DIR --text "the clip's exact words" --name NAME --out voices/NAME.json
@@ -61,6 +61,8 @@ tensorfold-native voice encode clip.wav --model DRAMA3_DIR --text "the clip's ex
 
 It runs the same encode in BF16 with the reference's roundings, in under 1 GB of device memory and about 4.5 s for a 50 s clip.
 Its codes are not yet identical to the reference's: on three clips 2.5 to 3 % of semantic codes and 3 to 14 % of residual codes differ.
+It resamples any common rate up to 384 kHz to 44.1 kHz.
+It refuses a rate whose resampling filter would pass 2048 taps or 2^20 coefficients (44056 Hz, for one), a truncated file or chunk, a partial sample frame and a malformed WAVE_FORMAT_EXTENSIBLE header.
 Run alone it writes no `source`, so `--voices` refuses the file; `drama_voice.py --native BINARY` runs it and adds one.
 
 A voice file is JSON with these fields:
